(4456342, 634984, 30982, '<td valign="top">Not sure if the guys below are teasing your or not, but I don''t think this would be nearly as good without the tourists.  Excellent shot and congratulations on the very high finish!</td>', 'Not sure if the guys below are teasing your or not, but I don''t think this would be nearly as good without the tourists.  Excellent shot and congratulations on the very high finish!', '2008-02-04 06:09:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(4456355, 636835, 30982, '<td valign="top">This is excellent.  And I like the idea of you running back and forth to set the timer, check the shot, set the timer...  And a really good composition.  ''Grats on the top ten!</td>', 'This is excellent.  And I like the idea of you running back and forth to set the timer, check the shot, set the timer...  And a really good composition.  ''Grats on the top ten!', '2008-02-04 06:24:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(4457545, 634457, 62787, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club. <br/> <br/>Wow, can I just say - I love this!!! Great simple framing and composition, and the slight imbalance in composition makes it seem pretty much as if the boat is rocking :) <br/> <br/>Strange patronising comment about how you should learn what a frame is? Ignore it. Obviously this is a frame, and obviously it meets the challenge. And a very nice frame too. <br/>As for the comments along the lines of - wouldn''t it be nice if it was a full-on pirate longboat having a marine fight with a large sea monster poking its head out just as you took the shot... Well, they stand :) That would be awesome. But what can you do.<br/> <br/>One thing I don''t like about the photo is that it strikes me as a bit too gray, I think it would look much nicer if it was lighter. Something that could easily be altered in pp, by lightening it using levels or brightness/contrast adjustments. Maybe a little bit more saturation would be nice as well (though not necessary).<br/> <br/>So yeah, I''d say overall the picture is very good (as you can tell from the score), but maybe tweaking with it a bit more in post-processing would have made it even better.<br/> <br/>The title... It''s a good idea not to hit the voters on the head with the challenge theme in the title, it should be obvious from the picture. I do like both the titles though :)<br/> <br/>Anyway, hope this helps. And good luck with future challenges!<br/> <br/>Jelena</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club.  Wow, can I just say - I love this!!! Great simple framing and composition, and the slight imbalance in composition makes it seem pretty much as if the boat is rocking :)  Strange patronising comment about how you should learn what a frame is? Ignore it. Obviously this is a frame, and obviously it meets the challenge. And a very nice frame too. As for the comments along the lines of - wouldn''t it be nice if it was a full-on pirate longboat having a marine fight with a large sea monster poking its head out just as you took the shot... Well, they stand :) That would be awesome. But what can you do. One thing I don''t like about the photo is that it strikes me as a bit too gray, I think it would look much nicer if it was lighter. Something that could easily be altered in pp, by lightening it using levels or brightness/contrast adjustments. Maybe a little bit more saturation would be nice as well (though not necessary). So yeah, I''d say overall the picture is very good (as you can tell from the score), but maybe tweaking with it a bit more in post-processing would have made it even better. The title... It''s a good idea not to hit the voters on the head with the challenge theme in the title, it should be obvious from the picture. I do like both the titles though :) Anyway, hope this helps. And good luck with future challenges! Jelena', '2008-02-04 14:41:52.000000', NULL, 0),
(4457766, 636730, 83773, '<td valign="top">i love this! awesome!!</td>', 'i love this! awesome!!', '2008-02-04 15:46:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(4457927, 635168, 62787, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Wow, your first challenge entry? Amazing capture! And great score too. Congratulations, and welcome to DPC!<br/> <br/>I think the picture is great - creative, effective take on the challenge, extremely engaging portraiture, with some very interesting colours and patterns. I love the lady''s steady gaze, and that slightly mysterious facial expression. And the interplay of light and shadow gives the picture a wonderful feeling of depth - the direction of the light works very well. <br/> <br/>Not entirely sure about the composition. While I think it adds to the picture to have the folds of the scarf included as they are, that lovely engaging gaze seems rather awkwardly positioned at the top of the picture. I would''ve ideally maybe wanted to see it on one of the thirds lines, so it''s possible that a tighter crop would work better? Not sure how well it would fit the challenge in that case though...<br/> <br/>As has already been mentioned, the lighting is harsh. While I love the direction of the light you used, the harshness of it seems to result in some uncomfortable glare on the eyes, nose and chin, and also seems to make the model''s every pore stand out. Maybe bouncing the light off a white piece of cardboard or a circular reflector could have accomplished a similar, but more gentle, flattering picture. <br/> <br/>I think the focus works reasonably well. It might be nice if both the eyes were perfectly in focus, but I do think the picture works as it is. Advanced editing rules provide much more maneuvering space for post-processing modifications, which would''ve worked well for the image, but wouldn''t have been allowed in this challenge, so I guess there''s not much point in commenting on them... In terms of what you could''ve done in basic: I think the image would benefit from being a bit more contrasty, and maybe a warming filter to get rid of that bluish tint on her skin (if you were shooting in RAW, you could fix this by tweaking with the white balance).<br/> <br/>Anyway, hope that helps. But well done, I think it''s an excellent shot, and I look forward to seeing more entries from you in the future!<br/> <br/>Any questions - just message me!<br/> <br/>Jelena</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Wow, your first challenge entry? Amazing capture! And great score too. Congratulations, and welcome to DPC! I think the picture is great - creative, effective take on the challenge, extremely engaging portraiture, with some very interesting colours and patterns. I love the lady''s steady gaze, and that slightly mysterious facial expression. And the interplay of light and shadow gives the picture a wonderful feeling of depth - the direction of the light works very well.  Not entirely sure about the composition. While I think it adds to the picture to have the folds of the scarf included as they are, that lovely engaging gaze seems rather awkwardly positioned at the top of the picture. I would''ve ideally maybe wanted to see it on one of the thirds lines, so it''s possible that a tighter crop would work better? Not sure how well it would fit the challenge in that case though... As has already been mentioned, the lighting is harsh. While I love the direction of the light you used, the harshness of it seems to result in some uncomfortable glare on the eyes, nose and chin, and also seems to make the model''s every pore stand out. Maybe bouncing the light off a white piece of cardboard or a circular reflector could have accomplished a similar, but more gentle, flattering picture.  I think the focus works reasonably well. It might be nice if both the eyes were perfectly in focus, but I do think the picture works as it is. Advanced editing rules provide much more maneuvering space for post-processing modifications, which would''ve worked well for the image, but wouldn''t have been allowed in this challenge, so I guess there''s not much point in commenting on them... In terms of what you could''ve done in basic: I think the image would benefit from being a bit more contrasty, and maybe a warming filter to get rid of that bluish tint on her skin (if you were shooting in RAW, you could fix this by tweaking with the white balance). Anyway, hope that helps. But well done, I think it''s an excellent shot, and I look forward to seeing more entries from you in the future! Any questions - just message me! Jelena', '2008-02-04 16:44:27.000000', NULL, 0),
(4461718, 635515, 61961, '<td valign="top">Very nice.  Congrats on making the top 20!</td>', 'Very nice.  Congrats on making the top 20!', '2008-02-05 23:10:35.000000', NULL, 0),
(4469192, 635715, 59029, '<td valign="top">I think it''s a very interesting shot and shows use of thirds, which is always good.  Hint: kill the camera-mounted flash.  It flattens the depth of field.  Indirect lighting, ie one on either side of what you''re shooting, will help eliminate shadows and add depth to your pictures.  Good try!</td>', 'I think it''s a very interesting shot and shows use of thirds, which is always good.  Hint: kill the camera-mounted flash.  It flattens the depth of field.  Indirect lighting, ie one on either side of what you''re shooting, will help eliminate shadows and add depth to your pictures.  Good try!', '2008-02-07 19:22:32.000000', NULL, 0),
(4490279, 635715, 89427, '<td valign="top">lame</td>', 'lame', '2008-02-16 08:42:47.000000', NULL, 0),
(4494207, 637019, 30982, '<td valign="top">I quite like that you got the whole cat in the shot, and it is indeed effectively framed.  The yellow cast, indicative of "indoor" lighting, kinda matches his eyes.  Pretty cat, too!</td>', 'I quite like that you got the whole cat in the shot, and it is indeed effectively framed.  The yellow cast, indicative of "indoor" lighting, kinda matches his eyes.  Pretty cat, too!', '2008-02-18 10:05:02.000000', NULL, 0),
